{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=501 && NR<=1000 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\"","aggregated_output":"   501\t// inference/startup-validation.ts — AC-5.1, 5.2, 5.3\n   502\texport interface ValidationReport {\n   503\t  unreachable: {\n   504\t    kind: FormKind;\n   505\t    provider: string;\n   506\t    model: string;\n   507\t    reason: \"unknown_model\" | \"auth_not_configured\";\n   508\t    fix: string;\n   509\t  }[];\n   510\t}\n   511\texport function validateReachable(\n   512\t  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>,\n   513\t  ctx: ExtensionContext,\n   514\t): ValidationReport;\n   515\texport function report(\n   516\t  r: ValidationReport,\n   517\t  ctx: ExtensionContext,\n   518\t  state: SessionState,\n   519\t): void; // ctx.ui when available + structured state diagnostic\n   520\t\n   521\t// lifecycle/fork.ts — AC-3.1, 3.2, 3.3\n   522\texport interface ForkInfo { sourceFile: string; forkEntryId: string; }\n   523\texport function detectFork(ctx: ExtensionContext): ForkInfo | null;\n   524\texport function seedFork(\n   525\t  source: ThreadRef, target: ThreadRef, forkPoint: string,\n   526\t): Promise<OpResult<void>>;\n   527\t\n   528\t// verify/replay.ts — AC-6.1, 6.2\n   529\texport interface ReplayResult { matches: boolean; diff?: string; }\n   530\texport function replayCorpus(corpus: Corpus, threadRef: ThreadRef): Promise<ReplayResult>;\n   531\t```\n   532\t\n   533\t---\n   534\t\n   535\t## Architecture-Risk Tests\n   536\t\n   537\tBeyond the TC→test mapping, the chosen architecture creates hazards the AC/TC mapping alone would miss. These are non-TC tests assigned to chunks.\n   538\t\n   539\t| Risk | Test file | Test | Why AC/TC mapping alone would miss it |\n   540\t|------|-----------|------|---------------------------------------|\n   541\t| Persistence / Restart | `lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts` | Discard the in-memory holder, re-resolve the same thread by its id from the registry, capture continues on it | The AC says \"resume on the resolved thread\"; only a real reopen proves resolution works across process death without a retained object |\n   542\t| Idempotency / Retry | `capture/idempotency.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice produces no duplicate events; re-delivered keys come back `skipped` | TC checks dedup once; the hazard is re-delivery under reload/replay being safe |\n   543\t| Adapter / Runtime Boundary | `inference/model-call.test.ts` | Each PI/pi-ai failure shape maps to the exact `ModelCallFailureKind` | TC may say \"error classified\"; the hazard is the precise runtime-error → code mapping |\n   544\t| Atomicity / Isolation | `capture/converter.test.ts` | A mid-batch capture failure records a gap and continues; no exception reaches the hook; store-unavailable produces a health signal not a gap | The session continuing is observable; the no-exception-into-hook guarantee is not an AC |\n   545\t| Source vs Derived Truth | `lifecycle/fork.test.ts` | After fork, source thread read-back is unchanged and forms requeue on the target | The epic sees a correct fork; the hazard is the source being silently mutated by seeding |\n   546\t| Fixture Validity | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` | Corpus loader yields valid `MessageEventInput` shapes and lifecycle-coherent sequences | Fixture correctness is test substrate, not product behavior, so no AC covers it |\n   547\t| Concurrency / Lost Update | `inference/closed-loop.test.ts` | A stale background derivation result does not clobber newer thread state | LHC owns this, but the connector's background-mode wiring must not reintroduce it |\n   548\t\n   549\tMigration/compatibility is N/A — POC threads are out of scope (epic Migration), and the package is greenfield. Threshold/budget is N/A — visibility budgets are Epic 2.\n   550\t\n   551\t---\n   552\t\n   553\t## Conditional Design Sections\n   554\t\n   555\t### Fixture Contracts\n   556\t\n   557\tThe epic's verification flow and most capture tests run on fixtures, so fixture validity is a foundation invariant. Two fixture families:\n   558\t\n   559\t- **Recorded corpora** (`test/fixtures/corpus.ts`) — the M0 captures, loaded and validated to produce well-formed `MessageEventInput` sequences. Each corpus is a named lifecycle (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn). Default loaders represent valid captures; an explicitly named `makeTruncatedCorpus()` produces a deliberately malformed sequence for the capture-failure test.\n   560\t- **Synthetic builders** (`test/fixtures/synthetic.ts`) — `makeUserMessage`, `makeAssistantMessage({ thinking?, text?, toolCalls? })`, `makeToolResult({ id, isError? })`, and event-stream builders for `session_start`/`agent_end` etc. These drive the lifecycle and turn-derivation tests where a precise hand-built shape beats a recorded one.\n   561\t- **Deterministic ModelCall fakes** (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`) — `fakeModelCallText(text)`, `fakeModelCallFailure(kind)`, and a per-kind router for multi-lane tests.\n   562\t\n   563\tFoundation tests (Chunk 0) verify these invariants before behavior chunks consume them.\n   564\t\n   565\t### Derived-State Provenance\n   566\t\n   567\tThis epic produces derived forms only via the closed-loop proof and fork requeue; it does not own provenance design (LHC does). The one connector-side rule: the fork path must not copy derived forms (I-2), so a forked thread's forms are always freshly derived and carry the new thread's provenance, never the source's. No provenance fields are added by the connector.\n   568\t\n   569\t### Mutation / Lease Semantics\n   570\t\n   571\tThe connector does not mutate persisted thread state directly — all writes go through `intakeStream.messageEvents`, which LHC serializes per thread file. There is one writer per thread (the session's instance), single-process. The connector adds no locks or leases; it relies on LHC's per-file transaction semantics. The only stale-write surface is the background scheduler, which LHC owns; the connector's responsibility is limited to constructing in background mode and never also driving `drain` (which would create a second writer).\n   572\t\n   573\t### Deterministic Algorithm Boundaries\n   574\t\n   575\tTwo deterministic mechanics need golden cases:\n   576\t\n   577\t- **Turn derivation** — exact inputs (a PI event sequence) to exact outputs (the ordered LHC events plus the single `turn_end`). The worked example in Flow 2 is the first golden case; others cover an aborted run (turn closes complete-but-aborted at `agent_end` with partial content) and a hard-kill (no `agent_end` → open turn left open, tolerated as a no-op on reattach).\n   578\t- **Replay equality** — a corpus replayed twice yields byte-identical read-back. Golden cases pin the deterministic-ID property end to end through the converter.\n   579\t\n   580\tIdempotency-key construction is deterministic but its golden cases live with the idempotency risk test rather than here.\n   581\t\n   582\t---\n   583\t\n   584\t## Testing Strategy\n   585\t\n   586\tThe test architecture follows directly from where this component's real boundaries are. The connector's own modules — converter, mapper, accumulator, model-call, validation — are never mocked against each other; they are exercised through their entry points (the hook handlers, the converter, the replay harness) so wiring bugs between them surface. Mocks live only at the two true external edges.\n   587\t\n   588\t```\n   589\t        /\\\n   590\t       /  \\   Manual: load into real PI, run a session, inspect the thread\n   591\t      /----\\\n   592\t     /      \\  Entry-point tests: hook handlers / converter / replay\n   593\t    /--------\\  driven by synthetic events + corpora, real temp SQLite\n   594\t   /          \\ Module tests: map-message, turn-accumulator, idempotency,\n   595\t  /------------\\ classifyFailure — pure, no mocks\n   596\t /--------------\\\n   597\t```\n   598\t\n   599\t**The critical mocking rule, applied:** mock at the external boundary, never at internal module boundaries. The external boundaries are the PI hook surface (fed by synthetic events and recorded corpora) and the `ModelCall`'s reach into PI's `modelRegistry` + pi-ai `complete` (supplied as a deterministic fake). Everything LHC runs real.\n   600\t\n   601\t| Layer | Mock? | Why |\n   602\t|-------|-------|-----|\n   603\t| PI hook events | Fed, not mocked | Synthetic builders + recorded corpora are the input, not a mocked dependency |\n   604\t| `modelRegistry` + pi-ai `complete` | Yes (the fake `ModelCall`) | External boundary — control completion text and failure shapes |\n   605\t| LHC SDK (`intakeStream`, `threads`, scheduler, `inspect`) | **No** | The engine under test; runs real |\n   606\t| SQLite thread file | **No — real temp file** | Persistence, reattach, replay, fork are the product contract |\n   607\t| `ctx.ui` / config | Yes (injection) | No logic, just setup |\n   608\t| Connector's own modules | **No** | That's what's being tested |\n   609\t\n   610\t**Stateful local persistence:** reattach (TC-1.5), replay-equality (TC-6.2), and fork source-immutability (TC-3.1) all use real temp thread directories and prove behavior across a reopen or a second thread, because mocking the store would make these tests assert nothing.\n   611\t\n   612\tThe verification tiers are the `lhc` package's configured commands, which `pi-lhc` mirrors (pnpm workspace, vitest, tsc). What gets mocked appears again here intentionally; the same boundary is stated in External Contracts and in the matrix.\n   613\t\n   614\t---\n   615\t\n   616\t## Verification Scripts\n   617\t\n   618\tThese are the `pi-lhc` package's gates, matching the `lhc` package's configured composition (verified in `packages/lhc/package.json`). The `boundaries` step matters here because `check-boundaries.mjs` enforces the module-import rules that keep the connector's surfaces from leaking into each other.\n   619\t\n   620\t| Tier | Composition | Purpose |\n   621\t|------|-------------|---------|\n   622\t| `red-verify` | `build && typecheck && lint && boundaries` | TDD Red exit — everything except behavior tests |\n   623\t| `verify` | `red-verify && vitest run` | Standard development gate |\n   624\t| `green-verify` | `verify && check-test-immutability` | TDD Green exit — tests pass + immutability guard |\n   625\t| `verify-all` | `verify` (no separate integration/e2e suite yet) | Deep gate; labeled below |\n   626\t\n   627\tThere is no separate integration/e2e suite in this package yet. `verify-all` equals `verify` for now; the wide \"load into real PI\" pass is the Manual Verification step, not an automated suite. The test plan labels every suite `ran` / `absent` so `verify-all` does not imply coverage it lacks.\n   628\t\n   629\t---\n   630\t\n   631\t## Work Breakdown: Chunks and Phases\n   632\t\n   633\tChunks map to the epic's stories, with Chunk 0 carrying the shared foundation. Per-chunk Red/Green test tables and the full TC→test mapping live in `test-plan.md`; this section is the index.\n   634\t\n   635\t### Chunk 0: Foundation (always first)\n   636\t\n   637\tArchetypes: **Pure Type Foundation** (the interfaces above), **Fixture/Test Foundation** (corpus loader, synthetic builders, ModelCall fakes, temp-thread factory), **Orchestration/Command Foundation** (the extension entry point and hook-registration rail with structured-result stubs), and **Verification Gate Foundation** (package scripts/config for the gates named here).\n   638\t\n   639\t| Deliverable | Path | Contains |\n   640\t|-------------|------|----------|\n   641\t| Types | `src/**/(*.ts type exports)` | All interfaces from Interface Definitions |\n   642\t| Extension shell | `src/index.ts` | Hook registration; routes to stubbed handlers; holds no state |\n   643\t| State holder | `src/lifecycle/state.ts` | Plain-data `SessionState` |\n   644\t| Fixtures | `test/fixtures/{corpus,synthetic,model-call}.ts` | Valid-default builders + named invalid builders |\n   645\t| Temp-thread factory | `test/fixtures/thread.ts` | Real temp SQLite thread per test |\n   646\t| Package verification config | `package.json`, `tsconfig*.json`, test/lint scripts | `red-verify`, `verify`, `green-verify`, `verify-all`, boundary/lint wiring |\n   647\t\n   648\t**Exit criteria:** package verification scripts exist and run; `red-verify` passes; fixture-invariant tests pass (corpus shapes valid, synthetic builders produce coherent sequences, temp-thread factory creates and reopens a thread); the extension loads and registers hooks (smoke).\n   649\t\n   650\t### Chunk 1 — Session Lifecycle and Launch-Driven Thread Resolution (Story 1)\n   651\t\n   652\t**Scope:** instance construct/dispose, launch-driven resolution (new / `--session` partial-id / `--continue` / `--resume` picker), reload re-resolution, plain-data rule. **ACs:** 1.1–1.7. **Relevant sections:** Flow 1, `lifecycle/*`, Persistence/Restart risk. **Architecture-risk tests:** re-resolve-after-discard. **Substrate maturity:** depends on the registry `cwd` column, partial-id `resolve`, and cwd-filtered `listThreads` (epic A-8) — **Epic 1 LHC-side scope, gated before AC-1.6/1.7, not optional**. These are built first in this chunk; AC-1.7's cwd-scoped picker does not pass by degrading to an unscoped or untitled list. **Risk shape:** launch state machine; registry is the catalog; unresolvable id fails loud; no second writer.\n   653\t\n   654\t### Chunk 2 — Event Capture and Turn Derivation (Story 2)\n   655\t\n   656\t**Scope:** the converter, fan-out, turn derivation, runtime metadata capture, parallel correlation, error/abort capture, dedup, failure isolation. **ACs:** 2.1–2.8. **Relevant sections:** Flow 2, `capture/*`, Idempotency + Atomicity risks, Deterministic Boundaries (turn derivation). **Architecture-risk tests:** idempotency double-replay, mid-batch failure isolation. **Risk shape:** the highest-risk chunk; turn derivation is the load-bearing mechanic; capture failure must isolate.\n   657\t\n   658\t### Chunk 3 — Capture Verification (Story 3)\n   659\t\n   660\t**Scope:** corpus replay, read-back compare, deterministic re-replay, inspect surfacing. **ACs:** 6.1–6.3. **Relevant sections:** Flow 6, `verify/replay.ts`, Fixture Validity risk. **Substrate maturity:** consumes Chunk 2's converter through the real intake path, not a seam; consumes M0 corpora as fixtures (widens as M0 delivers).\n   661\t\n   662\t### Chunk 4 — Fork as New Thread (Story 4)\n   663\t\n   664\t**Scope:** fork detection (hook + Epic-1 session-tree fallback), replay-seed, source immutability, requeue. **ACs:** 3.1–3.3. **Relevant sections:** Flow 3, `lifecycle/fork.ts`, Source-vs-Derived risk. **Architecture-risk tests:** source read-back unchanged after seeding. **Risk shape:** new-thread authority; source is read-only; forms requeue (no copy); fork lineage's permanent home is LHC metadata (Epic 2+), not PI's session tree.\n   665\t\n   666\t### Chunk 5 — Inference Host Routing (Story 5)\n   667\t\n   668\t**Scope:** the `ModelCall`, multi-lane routing, classification, and the closed-loop proof. **ACs:** 4.1–4.5. **Relevant sections:** Flow 4, `inference/model-call.ts`, Adapter-Boundary + Concurrency risks. **Substrate maturity:** AC-4.5 consumes Chunk 2's captured content through the real scheduler + `drainSettled`, not a fake derivation. **Architecture-risk tests:** failure-shape→kind mapping, stale-result no-clobber.\n   669\t\n   670\t### Chunk 6 — Startup Validation and Assignment Config (Story 6)\n   671\t\n   672\t**Scope:** shape validation (LHC-enforced), reachability probe using existence + configured-auth checks, `ctx.ui`/headless reporting, config override. **ACs:** 5.1–5.5. **Relevant sections:** Flow 5, `inference/{assignments,startup-validation}.ts`. **Risk shape:** validation independent of capture; reporting must not assume a TUI.\n   673\t\n   674\t### Chunk Dependencies\n   675\t\n   676\t```\n   677\tChunk 0 (Foundation)\n   678\t   ├──> Chunk 1 (Lifecycle) ──> Chunk 2 (Capture) ──> Chunk 3 (Verification)\n   679\t   │                                     └──────────> Chunk 4 (Fork)\n   680\t   │                                     └──────────> Chunk 5 (Inference 4.5)\n   681\t   └──> Chunk 5 (Inference 4.1–4.4) ──> Chunk 6 (Validation)\n   682\t```\n   683\t\n   684\tChunk 5 splits across the diagram because the function and classification (4.1–4.4) depend only on the instance (Chunk 1 via 0), while the closed-loop proof (4.5) needs captured content (Chunk 2).\n   685\t\n   686\t---\n   687\t\n   688\t## Open Questions\n   689\t\n   690\t| # | Question | Owner | Blocks | Resolution |\n   691\t|---|----------|-------|--------|------------|\n   692\t| Q1 | Does M0 extend the intake schema for images/file-refs, or is degrade-to-`runtime_note` the final v1 answer? | M0 / Lee | Chunk 2 (final form) | Pending; safe default (I-6) unblocks design |\n   693\t| Q2 | Is the `initLhc` rename landed before implementation, or does Chunk 0 write against `createSdk` and rename later? | Lee | Chunk 0 import names | Pending; mechanical either way (A-4) |\n   694\t| Q3 | Final breadth of M0 corpora for Chunk 3 fixtures | M0 / Lee | Chunk 3 coverage | Pending; machinery builds against available corpora |\n   695\t\n   696\t---\n   697\t\n   698\t## Deferred Items\n   699\t\n   700\t| Item | Related AC | Reason deferred | Future work |\n   701\t|------|-----------|-----------------|-------------|\n   702\t| Fork derived-form reuse (copy instead of requeue) | AC-3.3 | Needs a provenance-identity safety check that does not exist; requeue is correct and cheap | Optimization once provenance identity is defined (Epic 2+) |\n   703\t| `thinkingSignature` / `thoughtSignature` re-injection | TDQ-3 | Capture drops them; re-injection is a serving concern | Epic 2 (serving) decides if signatures must round-trip |\n   704\t| Image/file-reference intake payload kinds | A-7 | M0 decision; degrade-to-`runtime_note` is the interim contract | Converter gains kinds if M0 extends the schema |\n   705\t\n   706\t---\n   707\t\n   708\t## Related Documentation\n   709\t\n   710\t- Epic: `epic.md`\n   711\t- Test plan: `test-plan.md`\n   712\t- Tech architecture: `../01-tech-arch.md`\n   713\t- Wiring research: `../notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=1 && NR<=500 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=1 && NR<=500 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Epic 1: Connector Core — Test Plan\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status:** Draft for review\n     4\t**Tech design:** `tech-design.md`\n     5\t**Epic:** `epic.md`\n     6\t\n     7\tThis plan maps every TC to one or more tests, assigns each to a chunk, and lays out the Red/Green sequence per chunk. The mock boundary is fixed by the tech design: the PI hook surface (fed by synthetic events and recorded corpora) and the `ModelCall` (a deterministic fake) are the only external edges; everything LHC runs real against a temp SQLite thread. Connector modules are exercised through their entry points, never mocked against each other.\n     8\t\n     9\t---\n    10\t\n    11\t## Test Count Reconciliation\n    12\t\n    13\t| Chunk | Story | TCs covered | TC tests | Architecture-risk tests | Fixture/foundation tests | Chunk total |\n    14\t|-------|-------|-------------|----------|-------------------------|--------------------------|-------------|\n    15\t| 0 | Foundation | — | 0 | 0 | 7 | 7 |\n    16\t| 1 | Lifecycle | TC-1.1…1.7 | 7 | 1 (restart) | 0 | 8 |\n    17\t| 2 | Capture | TC-2.1…2.9 | 9 | 2 (idempotency, atomicity) | 0 | 11 |\n    18\t| 3 | Verification | TC-6.1…6.3 | 3 | 1 (fixture validity) | 0 | 4 |\n    19\t| 4 | Fork | TC-3.1…3.3 | 3 | 1 (source vs derived) | 0 | 4 |\n    20\t| 5 | Inference | TC-4.1…4.5 | 5 | 2 (adapter boundary, concurrency) | 0 | 7 |\n    21\t| 6 | Validation | TC-5.1…5.6 | 6 | 0 | 0 | 6 |\n    22\t| **Total** | | **33 TCs** | **33** | **7** | **7** | **47** |\n    23\t\n    24\tCross-checks: 33 TC tests = the 33 TCs in the epic traceability table (one test minimum per TC). 7 architecture-risk tests = the 7 rows in the tech design's Architecture-Risk table. 7 foundation tests = Chunk 0's fixture/smoke/gate invariants, including package verification-script availability. Per-chunk totals sum to 47; the epic's story estimate range (59–74) is wider because it counts finer-grained sub-cases the implementer adds at Green — this plan counts the floor (one test per TC + named risk/foundation tests), not the ceiling.\n    25\t\n    26\t---\n    27\t\n    28\t## TC → Test Mapping\n    29\t\n    30\tGrouped by test file, since each row becomes a Red-phase test. All paths under `packages/pi-lhc/`.\n    31\t\n    32\t### `test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts`\n    33\t\n    34\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    35\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    36\t| TC-1.1 | New launch (no flag) creates one background instance + registered thread | `launch={}`, temp registry+dir | one thread file exists; registry row carries cwd; `resolve` returns it; instance in background mode; queue not driven by connector |\n    37\t| TC-1.4 | Shutdown disposes with flush; re-resolved thread complete | capture N events, then `session_shutdown` | re-resolving the thread reads all N events through last pre-shutdown event; no trailing loss |\n    38\t\n    39\t### `test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts`\n    40\t\n    41\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    42\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    43\t| TC-1.2 | Resolved launch attaches existing thread; no second thread | a registered thread; `launch={session:id}` | `threads.resolve` returns the same threadId; thread count unchanged |\n    44\t| TC-1.3 | Plain-data holder survives context replacement | fire two hooks with different `ctx` objects | capture continues using fresh ctx; holder never references a prior ctx (asserted by shape) |\n    45\t| TC-1.5 *(risk: restart)* | Reload re-resolves the thread by id after holder discarded | create, **discard in-memory holder**, fire `session_start{reload}` | thread reference rebuilt by resolving the id from the registry; capture continues on same thread |\n    46\t| TC-1.6 | Launch modes resolve correctly | `launch` = `{}` / `{session:fullId}` / `{session:partialId}` / `{continue:true}` / `{session:badId}` | new creates; full+partial id resolve the right thread; continue resolves most-recent; bad id returns an actionable error and creates **no** thread |\n    47\t| TC-1.7 | `--resume` picker lists cwd-scoped, resolves selection, empty-safe | threads in two cwds; `launch={resume:true}` | list shows only current-cwd threads with title+created; selection resolves; empty cwd returns null, no failure |\n    48\t\n    49\t### `test/capture/converter.test.ts`\n    50\t\n    51\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    52\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    53\t| TC-2.1 | User/assistant/toolResult record ordered; assistant fans out thinking→text→toolCall | synthetic sequence | events recorded in source order; assistant fan-out order exact |\n    54\t| TC-2.8 *(risk: atomicity)* | Malformed→gap vs store-unavailable→health signal | (a) malformed on writable thread; (b) unavailable store | (a) durable queryable gap; (b) extension health signal in `SessionState.health`, no gap, no hook exception |\n    55\t| TC-2.9 | Model/thinking changes captured as ordered runtime_notes | fire `model_select` then `thinking_level_select` mid-sequence | each records one `runtime_note` carrying new+previous value; order preserved relative to surrounding messages |\n    56\t\n    57\t### `test/capture/turn-derivation.test.ts`\n    58\t\n    59\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    60\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    61\t| TC-2.2 | One-prompt-two-tools-then-answer → exactly one LHC turn, `turn_end` at agent_end | the Flow 2 worked-example corpus | exactly one `turn_end`; emitted at `agent_end`; per-step PI `turn_end`s produce none |\n    62\t| TC-2.3 | Session order from converter source-event order, not turnIndex | two agent runs each starting turnIndex 0 | turns ordered by source-event order through the converter; no collision from repeated turnIndex 0 |\n    63\t\n    64\t### `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts`\n    65\t\n    66\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    67\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    68\t| TC-2.4 | Parallel tool calls, out-of-order completion, correlate by id | assistant with toolCalls a,b; results arrive b,a | each `tool_result` correlated to its call by `toolCallId`, not arrival order |\n    69\t| TC-2.5 | Error tool result captured with flag + content | toolResult `isError:true` | `tool_result` event with error flag and content; nothing dropped |\n    70\t| TC-2.7 *(risk: idempotency)* | Re-delivered event dedups; normal resume re-fires nothing | replay same event twice; separately, resume | re-delivery returns `skipped`/`duplicate_idempotency_key`; resume produces no historical re-fire |\n    71\t\n    72\t### `test/capture/abort.test.ts`\n    73\t\n    74\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    75\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    76\t| TC-2.6 | Graceful interrupt: partial content + aborted disposition; turn closes complete-but-aborted | aborted-turn corpus (`stopReason:aborted`, agent_end fires) | partial assistant content recorded; aborted disposition carried; turn closed at agent_end |\n    77\t\n    78\t> Hard-kill (no `agent_end`) is covered as a deterministic-boundary golden in `turn-derivation.test.ts`: open turn left open, tolerated as a no-op on reattach (no duplication of the recorded prefix).\n    79\t\n    80\t### `test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts`\n    81\t\n    82\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    83\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    84\t| TC-3.1 *(risk: source-vs-derived)* | Fork creates new thread; source receives no writes | capture a thread, fork it | new thread created; source **logical read-back** (events/messages/turns) unchanged; fork point resolved from `session_before_fork` hook (PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback) |\n    85\t| TC-3.2 | Fork seeded by replay; read-back matches source through fork point | fork at a known entry | seeded thread read-back equals source read-back through the fork point |\n    86\t| TC-3.3 | Forms requeue on fork (v1: no copy) | fork a thread with ready forms | target's forms requeue and drain; not copied from source |\n    87\t\n    88\t### `test/inference/model-call.test.ts`\n    89\t\n    90\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n    91\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n    92\t| TC-4.1 | Function resolves (provider,model) via registry, returns text | fake registry + complete returning text | returns `{ok:true,text}` for a single-turn message list |\n    93\t| TC-4.2 | Two kinds → different (provider,model) both route in one session | two assignments, two fakes | each call routes by its keys; both succeed |\n    94\t| TC-4.3 *(risk: adapter boundary)* | Failure shapes map to exact kinds | inject auth, invalid-request, rate-limit, timeout, network, thrown | terminal: auth, invalid_request; retryable: rate_limit, timeout, network; thrown → other |\n    95\t| TC-4.4 | Resolved-but-no-output → `empty_output` by adapter (not host fn) | complete returns empty content | LHC adapter yields `empty_output`; host fn returns text-or-transport-failure only |\n    96\t\n    97\t### `test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts`\n    98\t\n    99\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n   100\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n   101\t| TC-4.5 | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes fn + persists outcome | captured thread; fakes (one text, one failure); `drainSettled` | at least one ready derived form + one classified failure, both queryable via inspect/health |\n   102\t| *(risk: concurrency)* | Stale background result does not clobber newer state | force a stale derivation result against advanced state | newer state wins; stale result discarded |\n   103\t\n   104\t### `test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts`\n   105\t\n   106\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n   107\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n   108\t| TC-5.1 | All seven assignments validated against registry before first use | seven assignments, registry | validation runs at session start, before any derivation |\n   109\t| TC-5.2 | Unreachable lane reported with kind+pair+fix; appears headless | two assignments: (a) `modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` fails; (b) model exists but `hasConfiguredAuth(model)`/`getAvailable()` says auth is absent; headless mode (`hasUI:false`) | report names kind, (provider,model), reason, and distinct fix (`fix assignment` vs `run login/configure auth`); emitted via headless channel and stored in `SessionState.health`, not only TUI |\n   110\t| TC-5.3 | Validation failure leaves capture running | an unreachable lane | capture continues; affected lane's derivations fail classified + queryable |\n   111\t\n   112\t### `test/inference/assignments.test.ts`\n   113\t\n   114\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n   115\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n   116\t| TC-5.4 | Seven kinds load (provider,model,prompt) with shipped defaults; prompt registered | default config | all seven resolve; each prompt names a registered prompt |\n   117\t| TC-5.5 | Override takes effect next start, no code change | config override one kind | next `session_start` uses the override |\n   118\t| TC-5.6 | Incomplete/unknown assignment fails loud at init | missing kind / unknown prompt / placeholder | construction throws actionable error; no placeholder masks it |\n   119\t\n   120\t### `test/verify/replay.test.ts`\n   121\t\n   122\t| TC | Test | Setup | Assert |\n   123\t|----|------|-------|--------|\n   124\t| TC-6.1 | Each corpus replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture | chatty, tool-heavy, parallel, error, aborted corpora | read-back (events/messages/turns) matches fixture expectation per corpus |\n   125\t| TC-6.2 | Same corpus replayed twice → identical read-back | one corpus, two replays | byte-identical read-back; IDs and order stable |\n   126\t| TC-6.3 | inspect overview/health reflect captured session | a captured thread with a gap + a failed form | counts, last position, gaps, failed derivations all visible |\n   127\t\n   128\t### `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` *(risk: fixture validity)*\n   129\t\n   130\t| Test | Assert |\n   131\t|------|--------|\n   132\t| Corpus loader yields valid MessageEventInput shapes + lifecycle-coherent sequences | every loaded corpus parses to well-formed events; named invalid builder produces the intended malformed shape |\n   133\t\n   134\t---\n   135\t\n   136\t## Per-Chunk Red/Green Sequence\n   137\t\n   138\tEach chunk: write skeletons (structured-result stubs for services/adapters/handlers; `NotImplementedError` for pure algorithms) → Red (tests written, failing for the right reason — never asserting on `NotImplementedError`) → Green (implement to pass). Exit each chunk on `green-verify`.\n   139\t\n   140\t### Chunk 0: Foundation\n   141\t\n   142\t- **Skeleton:** all interface exports (`tech-design.md` → Interface Definitions); `index.ts` hook registration with stubbed handlers; `state.ts` complete (plain data, no stub).\n   143\t- **Red:** fixture-invariant tests (corpus shapes, synthetic coherence, temp-thread create+reopen); extension-loads smoke; package verification scripts/config present.\n   144\t- **Green:** fixtures + loaders + temp-thread factory implemented; extension registers hooks; `red-verify`/`verify`/`green-verify`/`verify-all` scripts run.\n   145\t- **Exit:** `red-verify` green; 7 foundation tests pass.\n   146\t\n   147\t### Chunk 1: Lifecycle\n   148\t\n   149\t- **Skeleton:** `instance.ts`, `thread-resolution.ts`, `picker.ts` as structured-result stubs.\n   150\t- **Red:** TC-1.1–1.7 + restart risk test, failing.\n   151\t- **Green:** construct/dispose, launch resolution (new / `--session` partial-id / `--continue` / `--resume` picker), reload re-resolution, plain-data enforcement.\n   152\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 8 tests pass.\n   153\t\n   154\t### Chunk 2: Capture\n   155\t\n   156\t- **Skeleton:** `map-message.ts`, `turn-accumulator.ts`, `idempotency.ts` (`NotImplementedError`); `converter.ts` (structured result).\n   157\t- **Red:** TC-2.1–2.9 + idempotency + atomicity risk tests, failing. Turn-derivation goldens (worked example, abort, hard-kill) written here.\n   158\t- **Green:** mapping fan-out, turn accumulation, runtime-change capture (model/thinking → runtime_note), key construction, batch flush with failure isolation.\n   159\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 11 tests pass. Highest-risk chunk — turn derivation and dedup are the load-bearing mechanics.\n   160\t\n   161\t### Chunk 3: Verification\n   162\t\n   163\t- **Skeleton:** `verify/replay.ts` (structured compare).\n   164\t- **Red:** TC-6.1–6.3 + fixture-validity risk test, failing.\n   165\t- **Green:** replay through the real converter+intake path; read-back compare; inspect surfacing.\n   166\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 4 tests pass. Consumes M0 corpora; widens as they arrive.\n   167\t\n   168\t### Chunk 4: Fork\n   169\t\n   170\t- **Skeleton:** `fork.ts` (detect + seed, structured result).\n   171\t- **Red:** TC-3.1–3.3 + source-vs-derived risk test, failing.\n   172\t- **Green:** fork-point detection (`session_before_fork` hook; PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback), replay-seed, requeue, source read-only.\n   173\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 4 tests pass.\n   174\t\n   175\t### Chunk 5: Inference\n   176\t\n   177\t- **Skeleton:** `model-call.ts` (`createModelCall`, `classifyFailure`).\n   178\t- **Red:** TC-4.1–4.5 + adapter-boundary + concurrency risk tests, failing. TC-4.5 uses captured content from Chunk 2 + real scheduler + `drainSettled`.\n   179\t- **Green:** registry resolve + complete, classification, multi-lane, closed-loop wiring.\n   180\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 7 tests pass.\n   181\t\n   182\t### Chunk 6: Validation\n   183\t\n   184\t- **Skeleton:** `assignments.ts`, `startup-validation.ts`.\n   185\t- **Red:** TC-5.1–5.6, failing.\n   186\t- **Green:** shape validation (LHC-enforced at construction) + reachability probe through `modelRegistry.find` plus configured-auth check + `ctx.ui`/headless reporting + override.\n   187\t- **Exit:** `green-verify`; 6 tests pass.\n   188\t\n   189\t---\n   190\t\n   191\t## Suite Accounting\n   192\t\n   193\t| Suite | Status | Note |\n   194\t|-------|--------|------|\n   195\t| `vitest run` (unit + entry-point) | ran | the 44 tests above |\n   196\t| `red-verify` (build/typecheck/lint/boundaries) | ran | per chunk |\n   197\t| `check-test-immutability` | ran | Green exit |\n   198\t| integration / e2e | **absent** | no separate suite this epic; `verify-all` == `verify` |\n   199\t| Manual: load into real PI, run a session, inspect | manual | the wide pass; not automated, recorded in tech-design Testing Strategy |\n   200\t\n   201\tNo suite is skipped on auth availability — the closed-loop test (TC-4.5) uses a deterministic `ModelCall` fake, not a live provider, so it always runs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=174 && NR<=270 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=174 && NR<=270 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\"","aggregated_output":"   174\tThe two mock boundaries are explicit in the tree: `test/fixtures/corpus.ts` + `synthetic.ts` feed the PI hook edge, and `test/fixtures/model-call.ts` supplies the inference edge. No module under `src/` is mocked by another module under `src/` — the converter is tested through the capture entry point against a real temp thread, not in isolation with a mocked intake.\n   175\t\n   176\t### Module Responsibility Matrix\n   177\t\n   178\tThis is the rosetta stone: every AC has a home, and every module names the ACs it carries. If an AC is not here, it has no owner.\n   179\t\n   180\t| Module | Status | Responsibility | Depends on | ACs |\n   181\t|--------|--------|----------------|-----------|-----|\n   182\t| `index.ts` | NEW | Register hook handlers; route each hook to lifecycle/capture/inference; hold no state | all `src/` modules | (wiring) |\n   183\t| `lifecycle/instance.ts` | NEW | `initLhc` in background mode on start; dispose with flush on shutdown; reconstruct on reload | `lhc` | AC-1.1, AC-1.4, AC-1.5 |\n   184\t| `lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts` | NEW | Launch flags → registry resolution (new / `--session` full+partial / `--continue` most-recent); reload reconstructs from resolved id; unresolvable id fails loud | `lhc` threads | AC-1.2, AC-1.5, AC-1.6 |\n   185\t| `lifecycle/picker.ts` | NEW | `--resume` cwd-scoped thread list (title + created) and selection | `lhc` threads (`listThreads`) | AC-1.7 |\n   186\t| `lifecycle/state.ts` | NEW | Plain-data-only holder and extension diagnostics; never caches a PI context/session object across hooks | — | AC-1.3, AC-2.7 |\n   187\t| `lifecycle/fork.ts` | NEW | Detect fork from `session_before_fork` hook (PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback); new thread; replay-seed to fork point; never write source | `lhc` threads, capture | AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-3.3 |\n   188\t| `capture/converter.ts` | NEW | Orchestrate map→accumulate→batch; isolate capture failure to a gap/health signal | map-message, turn-accumulator, `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.1, AC-2.7 |\n   189\t| `capture/map-message.ts` | NEW | Pure map of one `AgentMessage` to ordered events; fan-out; parallel-tool correlation; error-result; abort; unsupported→runtime_note | idempotency | AC-2.1, AC-2.3, AC-2.4, AC-2.5 |\n   190\t| `capture/turn-accumulator.ts` | NEW | Track the open LHC turn; emit exactly one `turn_end` at `agent_end`; ignore per-step PI `turn_end` | — | AC-2.2 |\n   191\t| `capture/runtime-changes.ts` | NEW | Map `model_select` / `thinking_level_select` hooks to ordered `runtime_note` events (new + previous value) | `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.8 |\n   192\t| `capture/idempotency.ts` | NEW | Construct a stable key per event so re-delivery dedups | — | AC-2.6 |\n   193\t| `inference/model-call.ts` | NEW | Resolve (provider,model) via registry; complete; classify failures; multi-lane | PI modelRegistry, pi-ai | AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-4.3, AC-4.4 |\n   194\t| `inference/assignments.ts` | NEW | Load seven assignments from config; fail loud on missing/unknown/placeholder; override on next start | — | AC-5.4, AC-5.5 |\n   195\t| `inference/startup-validation.ts` | NEW | Probe each assignment with `modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` for existence and `hasConfiguredAuth(model)` / `getAvailable()` membership for configured auth; report unknown and not-logged-in lanes through `ctx.ui`/headless diagnostics; leave capture running | PI modelRegistry | AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-5.3 |\n   196\t| `verify/replay.ts` | NEW | Replay a corpus through the converter; compare read-back; deterministic | converter, `lhc` inspect | AC-6.1, AC-6.2 |\n   197\t| (closed-loop, in `model-call` + scheduler) | NEW | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes the function and records an outcome | `lhc` scheduler, inspect | AC-4.5 |\n   198\t| `inspect` reads (via `lhc`) | EXISTS | Surface counts, position, gaps, failures | `lhc` inspect | AC-6.3 |\n   199\t\n   200\t### Component Interaction\n   201\t\n   202\t```mermaid\n   203\tsequenceDiagram\n   204\t  participant PI\n   205\t  participant IDX as index.ts\n   206\t  participant LC as lifecycle\n   207\t  participant CV as converter\n   208\t  participant IS as lhc.intakeStream\n   209\t  participant SCH as lhc scheduler\n   210\t  participant MC as model-call\n   211\t  PI->>IDX: session_start\n   212\t  IDX->>LC: resolve/create thread, initLhc(background)\n   213\t  PI->>IDX: message_end / turn_end / agent_end\n   214\t  IDX->>CV: map + accumulate\n   215\t  CV->>IS: messageEvents(threadRef, events)\n   216\t  Note over SCH: background scheduler drains on its own\n   217\t  SCH->>MC: ModelCall(provider, model, messages)\n   218\t  MC->>PI: modelRegistry.find + complete\n   219\t  PI->>IDX: session_shutdown\n   220\t  IDX->>LC: dispose(flush)\n   221\t```\n   222\t\n   223\t---\n   224\t\n   225\t## Flow-by-Flow Design\n   226\t\n   227\tEach flow below opens with the context that makes its mechanics make sense, then a sequence diagram annotated with the ACs it satisfies, then a skeleton-requirements table and the TC approaches. The depth is uneven on purpose: turn derivation, idempotency, reattach, the inference adapter, and fork-replay carry the design's risk and get the most room; thread creation and config loading are mechanical and get less.\n   228\t\n   229\t### Flow 1: Session Lifecycle and Launch-Driven Thread Resolution\n   230\t\n   231\tA session starts and the connector must answer one question before anything else: which thread does this run record into? The answer comes from the launch, not from a recovered pointer — the LHC thread is the system of record and the registry resolves it from the operator's choice. No thread-selecting flag means a new thread (registered with its cwd). `--session <id>` resolves a named thread by full or partial id. `--continue`/`-c` resolves the most recently created thread. `--resume`/`-r` lists the cwd's threads and resolves the operator's pick. The connector then constructs the LHC instance in background scheduler mode against the resolved thread; derivations drain on their own and the connector never drives the queue.\n   232\t\n   233\tBecause the thread id is an input to the run, there is nothing to keep durable across an early crash and no two records that can disagree — the failure mode an earlier draft tried to insure against (a session-file pointer lost before flush) does not exist. An unresolvable `--session` id is an error, reported actionably; it never silently creates a new thread.\n   234\t\n   235\tThe plain-data-only rule is enforced structurally here. The connector's only retained state is a `{ threadRef, flags }` holder. It never stores a PI `ctx` or session-manager object between hooks, because PI replaces those objects on new/resume/fork and a stale reference is a crash waiting for the next transition. Each handler uses the fresh `ctx` PI passes it.\n   236\t\n   237\t```mermaid\n   238\tsequenceDiagram\n   239\t  participant OP as operator (launch)\n   240\t  participant LC as lifecycle\n   241\t  participant TH as lhc.threads + registry\n   242\t  OP->>LC: session_start (launch flags)\n   243\t  alt no flag\n   244\t    LC->>TH: newThread(filePath, cwd, title)\n   245\t    Note over LC,TH: AC-1.1 new thread, background mode\n   246\t  else --session id\n   247\t    LC->>TH: resolve(id, partial match)\n   248\t    Note over LC,TH: AC-1.6 named, fails loud if unresolvable\n   249\t  else --continue\n   250\t    LC->>TH: resolve most recent\n   251\t  else --resume / -r\n   252\t    LC->>TH: listThreads(cwd) — picker — resolve(id)\n   253\t    Note over LC,TH: AC-1.7 cwd-scoped, titled\n   254\t  end\n   255\t  Note over LC: holds only threadRef and flags — AC-1.3\n   256\t  OP->>LC: session_shutdown\n   257\t  LC->>TH: dispose(flush)\n   258\t  Note over LC: AC-1.4 complete through last event\n   259\t```\n   260\t\n   261\t**Skeleton requirements:**\n   262\t\n   263\t| What | Where | Stub signature | Stub semantics |\n   264\t|------|-------|----------------|----------------|\n   265\t| Construct/dispose | `lifecycle/instance.ts` | `initInstance(threadRef, config): Promise<Instance>` / `dispose(): Promise<void>` | structured result; throws nothing into hook |\n   266\t| Resolve thread from launch | `lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts` | `resolveThread(launch: LaunchFlags, pi: ExtensionAPI): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef>>` | registry resolve/create by mode; partial-id match; loud on unresolvable; structured `OpResult` |\n   267\t| Resume picker | `lifecycle/picker.ts` | `pickThread(cwd: string): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef \\| null>>` | cwd-scoped `listThreads`; selection; `null` on empty |\n   268\t| State holder | `lifecycle/state.ts` | `class SessionState { threadRef; flags }` | plain data; no PI refs |\n   269\t\n   270\tTC approaches (TC-1.1…TC-1.5) live in `test-plan.md`; each asserts on observable thread/mapping state through a real temp thread, with the lifecycle entry point driven by synthetic `session_start`/`shutdown` events. The reattach test (TC-1.5) reconstructs from the durable record after discarding the in-memory holder — the architecture-risk test for restart survival.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=127 && NR<=230 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"awk 'NR>=127 && NR<=230 {printf \\\"%6d\\\\t%s\\\\n\\\", NR, \"'$0}'\"' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\"","aggregated_output":"   127\t## Module Boundaries\n   128\t\n   129\t### Top-Tier Surfaces\n   130\t\n   131\tThe tech arch names five top-tier surfaces for the whole `pi-lhc` extension. This epic touches three; the other two are out of scope and named so the boundary is explicit.\n   132\t\n   133\t| Surface | This epic | What lands here |\n   134\t|---------|-----------|-----------------|\n   135\t| Session Lifecycle | **Yes** | Instance construction/disposal, launch-driven thread resolution, reload re-resolution, fork detection |\n   136\t| Event Capture | **Yes** | The converter (message mapping, turn derivation, batching, dedup), capture-failure isolation, capture verification |\n   137\t| Inference Host | **Yes** | The `ModelCall` implementation, assignment config, startup validation |\n   138\t| Context Serving | No (Epic 2) | The `context` hook and thread-view serving are not registered in this epic |\n   139\t| Surfaces (operator/agent) | No (Epic 3) | Commands and self-inspection tools beyond what verification reads |\n   140\t\n   141\tAll modules below nest within one of the three active surfaces. The package is a fresh scaffold (`packages/pi-lhc/` currently holds only `package.json`), so every module is NEW; the only EXISTS dependencies are the `lhc` workspace package and the PI/pi-ai packages.\n   142\t\n   143\t### Module Architecture\n   144\t\n   145\t```\n   146\tpackages/pi-lhc/\n   147\t  src/\n   148\t    index.ts                       NEW  extension entry — registers hooks, owns nothing else\n   149\t    lifecycle/\n   150\t      instance.ts                  NEW  initLhc construct/dispose; background mode\n   151\t      thread-resolution.ts         NEW  launch flags → registry (new / --session / --continue / --resume); partial-id match\n   152\t      picker.ts                    NEW  --resume cwd-scoped thread list + selection (thin pi-lhc UI)\n   153\t      state.ts                     NEW  the plain-data-only holder { threadRef, flags, health } (no PI objects)\n   154\t      fork.ts                      NEW  fork detection (session_before_fork hook; PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback) + replay seeding\n   155\t    capture/\n   156\t      converter.ts                 NEW  orchestrates map → accumulate → batch flush\n   157\t      map-message.ts               NEW  pure: AgentMessage → MessageEventInput[]\n   158\t      turn-accumulator.ts          NEW  open-turn state; deriveTurnClose\n   159\t      idempotency.ts               NEW  pure: stable per-event key construction\n   160\t    inference/\n   161\t      model-call.ts                NEW  ModelCall over modelRegistry + complete; failure classification\n   162\t      assignments.ts               NEW  config load + shape validation (the seven kinds)\n   163\t      startup-validation.ts        NEW  registry existence + auth-availability probe; reporting (ctx.ui/headless)\n   164\t    verify/\n   165\t      replay.ts                    NEW  corpus → converter → thread; read-back compare (test-facing)\n   166\t  test/\n   167\t    fixtures/\n   168\t      corpus.ts                    NEW  recorded-corpus loader (M0 fixtures)\n   169\t      synthetic.ts                 NEW  synthetic AgentMessage / event builders\n   170\t      model-call.ts                NEW  deterministic ModelCall fakes (text / each failure kind)\n   171\t    ...                            NEW  per-chunk test files (see test-plan.md)\n   172\t```\n   173\t\n   174\tThe two mock boundaries are explicit in the tree: `test/fixtures/corpus.ts` + `synthetic.ts` feed the PI hook edge, and `test/fixtures/model-call.ts` supplies the inference edge. No module under `src/` is mocked by another module under `src/` — the converter is tested through the capture entry point against a real temp thread, not in isolation with a mocked intake.\n   175\t\n   176\t### Module Responsibility Matrix\n   177\t\n   178\tThis is the rosetta stone: every AC has a home, and every module names the ACs it carries. If an AC is not here, it has no owner.\n   179\t\n   180\t| Module | Status | Responsibility | Depends on | ACs |\n   181\t|--------|--------|----------------|-----------|-----|\n   182\t| `index.ts` | NEW | Register hook handlers; route each hook to lifecycle/capture/inference; hold no state | all `src/` modules | (wiring) |\n   183\t| `lifecycle/instance.ts` | NEW | `initLhc` in background mode on start; dispose with flush on shutdown; reconstruct on reload | `lhc` | AC-1.1, AC-1.4, AC-1.5 |\n   184\t| `lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts` | NEW | Launch flags → registry resolution (new / `--session` full+partial / `--continue` most-recent); reload reconstructs from resolved id; unresolvable id fails loud | `lhc` threads | AC-1.2, AC-1.5, AC-1.6 |\n   185\t| `lifecycle/picker.ts` | NEW | `--resume` cwd-scoped thread list (title + created) and selection | `lhc` threads (`listThreads`) | AC-1.7 |\n   186\t| `lifecycle/state.ts` | NEW | Plain-data-only holder and extension diagnostics; never caches a PI context/session object across hooks | — | AC-1.3, AC-2.7 |\n   187\t| `lifecycle/fork.ts` | NEW | Detect fork from `session_before_fork` hook (PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback); new thread; replay-seed to fork point; never write source | `lhc` threads, capture | AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-3.3 |\n   188\t| `capture/converter.ts` | NEW | Orchestrate map→accumulate→batch; isolate capture failure to a gap/health signal | map-message, turn-accumulator, `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.1, AC-2.7 |\n   189\t| `capture/map-message.ts` | NEW | Pure map of one `AgentMessage` to ordered events; fan-out; parallel-tool correlation; error-result; abort; unsupported→runtime_note | idempotency | AC-2.1, AC-2.3, AC-2.4, AC-2.5 |\n   190\t| `capture/turn-accumulator.ts` | NEW | Track the open LHC turn; emit exactly one `turn_end` at `agent_end`; ignore per-step PI `turn_end` | — | AC-2.2 |\n   191\t| `capture/runtime-changes.ts` | NEW | Map `model_select` / `thinking_level_select` hooks to ordered `runtime_note` events (new + previous value) | `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.8 |\n   192\t| `capture/idempotency.ts` | NEW | Construct a stable key per event so re-delivery dedups | — | AC-2.6 |\n   193\t| `inference/model-call.ts` | NEW | Resolve (provider,model) via registry; complete; classify failures; multi-lane | PI modelRegistry, pi-ai | AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-4.3, AC-4.4 |\n   194\t| `inference/assignments.ts` | NEW | Load seven assignments from config; fail loud on missing/unknown/placeholder; override on next start | — | AC-5.4, AC-5.5 |\n   195\t| `inference/startup-validation.ts` | NEW | Probe each assignment with `modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` for existence and `hasConfiguredAuth(model)` / `getAvailable()` membership for configured auth; report unknown and not-logged-in lanes through `ctx.ui`/headless diagnostics; leave capture running | PI modelRegistry | AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-5.3 |\n   196\t| `verify/replay.ts` | NEW | Replay a corpus through the converter; compare read-back; deterministic | converter, `lhc` inspect | AC-6.1, AC-6.2 |\n   197\t| (closed-loop, in `model-call` + scheduler) | NEW | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes the function and records an outcome | `lhc` scheduler, inspect | AC-4.5 |\n   198\t| `inspect` reads (via `lhc`) | EXISTS | Surface counts, position, gaps, failures | `lhc` inspect | AC-6.3 |\n   199\t\n   200\t### Component Interaction\n   201\t\n   202\t```mermaid\n   203\tsequenceDiagram\n   204\t  participant PI\n   205\t  participant IDX as index.ts\n   206\t  participant LC as lifecycle\n   207\t  participant CV as converter\n   208\t  participant IS as lhc.intakeStream\n   209\t  participant SCH as lhc scheduler\n   210\t  participant MC as model-call\n   211\t  PI->>IDX: session_start\n   212\t  IDX->>LC: resolve/create thread, initLhc(background)\n   213\t  PI->>IDX: message_end / turn_end / agent_end\n   214\t  IDX->>CV: map + accumulate\n   215\t  CV->>IS: messageEvents(threadRef, events)\n   216\t  Note over SCH: background scheduler drains on its own\n   217\t  SCH->>MC: ModelCall(provider, model, messages)\n   218\t  MC->>PI: modelRegistry.find + complete\n   219\t  PI->>IDX: session_shutdown\n   220\t  IDX->>LC: dispose(flush)\n   221\t```\n   222\t\n   223\t---\n   224\t\n   225\t## Flow-by-Flow Design\n   226\t\n   227\tEach flow below opens with the context that makes its mechanics make sense, then a sequence diagram annotated with the ACs it satisfies, then a skeleton-requirements table and the TC approaches. The depth is uneven on purpose: turn derivation, idempotency, reattach, the inference adapter, and fork-replay carry the design's risk and get the most room; thread creation and config loading are mechanical and get less.\n   228\t\n   229\t### Flow 1: Session Lifecycle and Launch-Driven Thread Resolution\n   230\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
